00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:30.440
LAURA HAM: That's right.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:32.630
GABI FERRARA: So
for us, what it is--

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:33.620
Weaviate?

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:36.840
LAURA HAM: Yeah, so Weaviate
is a knowledge graph--

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:38.900
open source knowledge
graph, which

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:43.400
enables you to do contextualized
search based on your data

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:44.240
you put in.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:46.910
More specifically,
we have, instead

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:50.450
of a traditional database,
where data in a database

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:52.380
has no meaning at all--

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.880
so a database doesn't know what
kind of knowledge is in there--

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:58.310
we create meanings.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:00.500
We attach meanings to
the data, which you

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:01.750
put in the knowledge graphs.

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:03.600
This all goes automatically.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.560
And this enables
you to do search

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:10.130
based on this knowledge you
put in to gain more knowledge.

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:12.245
You can get more knowledge
out of it, let's say.

00:09:12.245 --> 00:09:14.360
GABI FERRARA: So how
do you query that data?

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:15.610
Like, you put the data there.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:16.860
It's, like, a knowledge graph.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:20.120
But how do you retrieve
the data from there?

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:23.180
It's almost like a
graph, right, as I said.

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:25.640
So I know some graph databases.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.040
But I don't think it's a
graph database, per se.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:29.020
Or is it?

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:30.978
LAURA HAM: It is not a
graph database, per se.

00:09:30.978 --> 00:09:31.520
That's right.

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:33.290
The structure is based
on a graph, though.

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:36.470
And we want to have a
big variety of users

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:39.920
being able to use and
query this graph, which

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:45.530
is why I designed a GraphQL API,
which is a way you can almost

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:48.830
use natural language
to query the API, which

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:50.508
is designed by ourselves.

00:09:50.508 --> 00:09:51.800
JON FOUST: That sounds amazing.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.157
I'm going to circle back on
the natural language part,

00:09:54.157 --> 00:09:55.490
because that's very interesting.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:57.240
But can you give me
example of a knowledge

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:00.020
graph, because I don't
particularly work in data--

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:02.630
like, a sneak peek of, you know,
someone who's using Weaviate

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:04.563
would use to actually build out?

00:10:04.563 --> 00:10:05.480
LAURA HAM: Yeah, sure.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:09.780
So we can take as an example,
countries and cities.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:12.680
So if we have the
country, Netherlands,

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:15.570
where I'm from with
the capital Amsterdam,

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:19.100
we have a clear link between
Amsterdam and the Netherlands,

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.410
because Amsterdam is the
capital of the Netherlands.

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:23.850
The knowledge graph
stores this data.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:26.560
It has a link between the
Netherlands and Amsterdam.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:29.160
And if we, then,
have, for example,

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.450
a second country in
there, which is Germany,

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:35.360
and we have a second city
in there, which is Berlin,

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.390
but we didn't make the link
yet between Berlin, which

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:42.380
is the capital of Germany,
we can automatically

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:45.630
classify this and make
the link to the capital.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:47.810
JON FOUST: Sounds like
a knowledge graph, too,

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:50.540
is they-- it starts off
with, I would say, a node,

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:52.110
like many graphs do.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:55.080
And it seems like once you
want to populate new data,

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:58.105
it just starts to bridge off
into different directions,

00:10:58.105 --> 00:10:59.480
almost like building
a map, like,

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:01.070
in this particular example.

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:03.620
And you're starting to
populate different nodes

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:05.150
with more and more information.

00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:07.182
And then, from each node,
it seems to spread--

00:11:07.182 --> 00:11:09.515
continue to spread, continue
to gather more information.

00:11:09.515 --> 00:11:12.830
So but it obviously
can be user defined,

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:14.760
or it can be
automatically done, right?

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:16.010
LAURA HAM: Yeah, that's right.

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.530
So data is stored
based on this meaning

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:21.200
instead of based
on nothing or based

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.450
on a number, which is in a
relational or traditional

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:24.440
database.

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:26.660
And that's the most
important difference for us.

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:30.470
And then, based on this
meaning, you can make new links

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:31.648
and extract new knowledge.

00:11:31.648 --> 00:11:32.190
That's right.

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:32.520
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:35.030
LAURA HAM: And that's all starts
with the nodes, as you say,

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:37.700
which we define as classes.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:39.880
So we have a class
country, a class city,

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:41.650
which has certain properties.

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:44.093
Country has a
property has capital.

00:11:44.093 --> 00:11:46.510
JON FOUST: And just to circle
back on the natural language

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:48.310
that you've been using, can
you tell us a little bit more

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:48.930
about that?

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:51.940
LAURA HAM: Yeah, so for
the GraphQL API, you mean?

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:52.680
JON FOUST: Yes.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:54.910
LAURA HAM: Yeah, for our
query language GraphQL,

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:58.910
we wanted to make it usable
for as many people as possible.

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:02.490
So yeah, for example, query
languages like Cypher or Neo4j,

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:05.230
but those are kind
of hard to understand

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:08.300
and not really intuitive if you
see them for the first time.

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:11.020
So what I've done
with GraphQL is

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:13.960
start with how you
would ask a database

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.570
or how you would ask
something with data in it

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:19.090
to get back what you
actually are looking for.

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:20.590
So I started from this basis.

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:24.040
And from there, I found that
some keywords were coming back

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:25.180
all the time.

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:26.800
If I can give an
example, so if you

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.320
know what you're looking
for, you very often

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:30.520
use the word "get"--

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:33.200
so can I get this
specific thing?

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.805
So that is why a very key
function in the GraphQL API

00:12:36.805 --> 00:12:38.320
is the get function.

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:39.790
So you start with get.

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:41.620
And then, you go on.

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:43.245
GABI FERRARA: You
mentioned the example

00:12:43.245 --> 00:12:47.060
of countries and capitals.

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:51.050
So what is the most common use
case, like, for a business,

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:53.470
like, that people
use it for that?

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:57.790
Like, that example was abstract,
but like, a real-world example

00:12:57.790 --> 00:12:59.450
that people are using this.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:01.420
LAURA HAM: So for example,
if we are going on

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:03.760
on this context-based
search, then

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:06.680
we can look into
scientific articles.

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:10.450
For example, if you have
a database of articles

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:13.580
and you want to look
for a specific topic,

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:16.690
then you might start
looking at some words, which

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:19.580
are not actually
present in this article.

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:23.710
So you might start looking for
some kind of specific disease.

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:27.160
But this disease is named
differently in an article.

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:29.450
And you still want
to find this article.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:32.500
And if we have all this
data in our knowledge graph,

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:35.350
then you could actually
find articles like this.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:38.560
So you search based on the
context instead of search

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:40.107
based on the exact keywords.

00:13:40.107 --> 00:13:40.690
JON FOUST: OK.

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:43.990
And that is pretty much
defined by each node

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:46.210
in your graph's actual data.

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:50.910
That is either supplied by a
user or built automatically.

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:52.030
LAURA HAM: Yeah, correct.

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:55.090
And the nice thing is is
that you give a structure

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:56.840
to your data yourself.

00:13:56.840 --> 00:13:59.830
So that's what we call
an ontology or a schema.

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:01.960
You define your own ontology.

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:04.690
But what happens is
if you put in data,

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:07.030
factors are generated
automatically

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:09.620
to represent the
meaning of these words.

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:13.180
And that is not only
depending your own ontology,

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:17.050
but that is based on what
we call Contextionary, which

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:19.570
is a vector representation
or machine learning

00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:23.320
model, which has learned on a
whole Wikipedia in Wiktionary.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:28.688
So it gives a meaning so that
others can also understand it.

00:14:28.688 --> 00:14:29.480
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.355
I'm going to pretend I
know what ontology is.

00:14:31.355 --> 00:14:34.430
But for our listeners, can you
give us a hardcore definition

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:35.330
of what ontology is?

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:36.800
LAURA HAM: [LAUGHS] Yeah, sure.

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:41.000
So an ontology is a description
of, well, the meaning

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.630
of the words basically.

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:49.200
So how we use ontology is we
define classes and properties.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:54.140
So as I said earlier, we have a
class, which can be a country.

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:56.990
And you describe
what country means.

00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:58.920
And a country can
have properties.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:00.530
So it can have a capital.

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:01.970
It has a population.

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:04.730
It might be in a
certain region--

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:06.650
whatever you want
to give to that.

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:10.190
And then, you can-- when you
populate your knowledge graph,

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:12.560
your database, and you
want to add a country,

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:16.580
you give the country,
Netherlands, the label Country.

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:18.710
GABI FERRARA: Can
a class reference

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:20.275
another class in the property?

00:15:20.275 --> 00:15:21.650
I'm going to give
you an example.

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:25.210
Like, let's say the
UK sends off countries

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:26.720
of their Commonwealth, right?

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:29.300
So let's say you
have a property,

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:32.450
which it is like empire
countries-- whatever--

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:33.350
because that's the--

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:38.450
UK has all the Great Britain
and I think Scotland and Wales.

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:41.990
So it's a country
inside other countries.

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:43.913
Could you do that, too?

00:15:43.913 --> 00:15:45.080
LAURA HAM: Yeah, yeah, sure.

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:48.950
So we have two types of
properties a class can have.

00:15:48.950 --> 00:15:50.760
One is just some data.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:54.220
So if you have the population of
a country, it's just a number.

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:56.480
But the example of
city that I gave,

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:57.780
the city is another class.

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:00.960
So you refer to this other
node in the database.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:03.390
And you can do the same
with other countries.

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:05.570
You can refer to a
same class again.

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:06.200
Yup.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:08.540
JON FOUST: I'm really, like,
interested in ontology.

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:09.623
I didn't know what it was.

00:16:09.623 --> 00:16:11.150
I'm just pretending
for our viewers.

00:16:11.150 --> 00:16:12.830
I really enjoyed
that definition,

00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:14.420
the meaning of words.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:16.130
I believe that's what you said.

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:20.060
So I guess to ask a follow-up,
can you give us, like,

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:24.020
a good example of, like, a
pretty, like, basic example

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:26.330
of the meaning of
words, meaning when

00:16:26.330 --> 00:16:30.170
you said that, I immediately
went back to my college days

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:32.660
when I took logical classes.

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:34.550
And everything was
pretty much quantitative.

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:37.640
Like, it's universal
or pretty much

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:42.020
like all things that are cute
are dogs, or all dogs are cute.

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:44.570
Or cats are boring, for example.

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:45.462
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:45.462 --> 00:16:48.107
[MEOW]

00:16:48.107 --> 00:16:49.940
LAURA HAM: Yeah, that's
a very good example,

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:51.810
what you just
mentioned, because it

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:55.890
is-- an ontology is something
everyone defines for itself.

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:59.150
So if we have a bank,
for example, the word

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:01.370
"bank," if I say
"bank," you might think

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:03.080
of a financial institution.

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:05.150
But another might
think of a river bank.

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:05.930
JON FOUST: Oh, OK.

00:17:05.930 --> 00:17:09.200
LAURA HAM: So that's why
we define the word "bank"

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:11.210
in ontology with a description.

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:14.510
And that's where it gets a
vector representation, which

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:15.810
represents its meaning.

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:16.940
So we have two banks.

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:19.910
And in a traditional database,
they would mean the same thing.

00:17:19.910 --> 00:17:22.470
But for us, we have
two different things.

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:24.710
And then, another good
point is what you said,

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:27.109
is that everyone
defines it differently.

00:17:27.109 --> 00:17:30.650
If we can use "bank" again, some
people might say it's a bank.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:33.590
But some people might say
it's a financial institution.

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:35.940
So people don't speak
the same language.

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:40.250
So that is why in our knowledge
graph, a bank is the same--

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:43.035
like, the financial institution
bank-- is the same thing.

00:17:43.035 --> 00:17:44.660
And then, we have
the other-- the river

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:46.080
bank as a separate thing.

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:49.580
So the knowledge graph does make
a difference between those two

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:50.340
words.

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:51.140
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:54.080
Ontology is based on
many different things.

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:57.360
Like, you know, people may
define banks different ways.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:17:58.880
So I have another question.

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:02.660
For ontology, it seems
like it's based on--

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:04.550
I won't say only
environment, but pretty

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:06.360
much everybody's way of living.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:09.685
So for example, if I were in
the Harry Potter universe--

00:18:09.685 --> 00:18:10.310
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:18:10.310 --> 00:18:14.130
--then my ontology of some
things might be different.

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:17.000
For example, like, medicine
might be different in the Harry

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:18.070
Potter universe.

00:18:18.070 --> 00:18:20.660
And they might have
different terminology.

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:23.120
But we might share
the same technology

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:27.170
in our non-fantasy world, where
medicine is just a little bit

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:29.150
more practical and scientific.

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:31.160
People are measuring and
testing and everything,

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:32.650
where with Harry
Potter, it's, I'm

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:34.517
going to throw this together.

00:18:34.517 --> 00:18:36.350
And if it works, it
works, because you know,

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:37.722
I've tested it once separately.

00:18:37.722 --> 00:18:39.680
And together, I believe
that this is the result

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:40.670
that it will have.

00:18:40.670 --> 00:18:42.860
It seems like ontology
is based solely

00:18:42.860 --> 00:18:45.960
on the actual surroundings
in the environment

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:49.250
and as well as people's
definitions and way of living.

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:51.067
Is that correct?

00:18:51.067 --> 00:18:52.400
LAURA HAM: Yeah, that's correct.

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:52.900
[DING]

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:56.210
And that is why everyone
has their own ontology.

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:58.190
I don't know if you
noticed, but people

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:00.845
have been trying to
make a formal ontology

00:19:00.845 --> 00:19:03.260
for, for example,
the web in the past.

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:06.740
And people just cannot
agree on a formal ontology,

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:08.750
because everyone's
meaning is different,

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:11.720
because it's based on their
own environment, as you say.

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:15.240
And that's exactly one of the
key points we want to avoid.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:17.660
So everyone can define
their own ontology

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:20.930
here so that they can
query and ask the knowledge

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:22.940
graph in their own language.

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:24.797
And we solved this
whole problem with,

00:19:24.797 --> 00:19:27.380
like, the machine learning model
we built within the knowledge

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:31.200
graph to still be able to make
the links between the thing you

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:33.570
mean and the thing I mean,
which is actually the same.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:34.940
But we just name it differently.

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:35.690
JON FOUST: Got it.

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:37.010
That's awesome.

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:39.720
GABI FERRARA: So to steer
back a bit that conversation,

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:42.150
your product helps solve a
lot of different problems

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:46.020
that people has, because usually
our common relational database

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:48.550
may not be what
they're looking for,

00:19:48.550 --> 00:19:49.750
although I still like them.

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:50.250
And--

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:52.115
