00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:05.370
to install your own
cluster and do everything?

00:23:05.370 --> 00:23:07.680
Like, well, how does it work?

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:10.630
LAURA HAM: Yeah, so Weaviate
is, indeed, open source.

00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:12.570
And it will always
stay open source.

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:14.905
But what we are doing
at SeMI Technologies

00:23:14.905 --> 00:23:17.130
is that we offer
our consulting help

00:23:17.130 --> 00:23:19.410
for getting started
with building

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:21.870
a whole Weaviate for companies.

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:24.560
And secondly, because
Weaviate is open source,

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:26.620
it comes with an
open source license.

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:30.690
And for bigger businesses,
we offer Weaviate Enterprise,

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:33.630
which comes with the
liability would go to us.

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:36.198
And that's what we're building
the business about basically.

00:23:36.198 --> 00:23:36.990
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:23:36.990 --> 00:23:39.660
We know based on, you
know, for example, like,

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:42.300
the success of Kubernetes,
being open source

00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:45.400
and then becoming managed as
GKE, we've seen the success.

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:49.200
So it's great to see that we
are contributing both to open

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:52.860
source, as well as offering
some liability and some support

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:54.300
for, you know, our users.

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:56.430
So it seems like
Weaviate is really making

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:58.092
a lot of strides in that area.

00:23:58.092 --> 00:24:00.300
LAURA HAM: If you look at
really popular applications

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:02.610
