00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:26.600
You want to enter into this
space, where Weaviate is

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:28.270
a go-to for knowledge graphs.

00:24:28.270 --> 00:24:29.380
But I'm curious.

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:32.140
For your Enterprise solution,
what are these companies

00:24:32.140 --> 00:24:33.670
using Weaviate
for, specifically,

00:24:33.670 --> 00:24:34.990
because you mentioned Uber?

00:24:34.990 --> 00:24:37.600
And I immediately thought,
oh, that's a good use

00:24:37.600 --> 00:24:39.580
of potential knowledge
graphs in Uber

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:41.110
or you know, something else.

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:43.150
So I'm just curious, in
your Enterprise Edition,

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:46.165
what are you seeing a lot
of use cases for Weaviate?

00:24:46.165 --> 00:24:48.040
LAURA HAM: There are
different possibilities,

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:50.260
because one of the
things we offer

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:53.180
due to our machine learning
model is classification.

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:55.150
So what we can,
for example, do is

00:24:55.150 --> 00:24:59.230
in the area of cybersecurity
is classifying emails

00:24:59.230 --> 00:25:01.870
if they are potential
threats or contains

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:04.540
phishing or not
based on the context

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:07.810
of the email and the knowledge,
which is already in your graph.

00:25:07.810 --> 00:25:09.780
And secondly, as
an Enterprise, you

00:25:09.780 --> 00:25:13.070
can gain knowledge out of your
data that you already have.

00:25:13.070 --> 00:25:16.100
So if you have a lot of
customer data, for example,

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:18.250
the knowledge graph
may be able to make

00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:20.575
new links between things
you hadn't thought about.

00:25:20.575 --> 00:25:21.700
And then, you're like, aha.

00:25:21.700 --> 00:25:23.980
Maybe this is a new
business case for us.

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:24.610
JON FOUST: OK.

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:27.520
So the machine learning
aspect of Weaviate

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:30.070
is actually like a
churn model, where

00:25:30.070 --> 00:25:33.640
it's pretty much identifying
different and new

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:35.560
potential business aspects.

00:25:35.560 --> 00:25:37.810
So Weaviate essentially
is just trying

00:25:37.810 --> 00:25:41.410
to connect your customers to
more areas of your business.

00:25:41.410 --> 00:25:44.950
And I'm assuming that in other
use cases or other companies,

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:48.760
it seems like it would
be connecting users

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:50.410
to your product a lot better.

00:25:50.410 --> 00:25:51.760
[LAUGHS]

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:55.250
LAURA HAM: Yeah, as Weaviate,
we stop also, because of this,

00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:56.540
at the API level.

00:25:56.540 --> 00:26:00.490
So we will never build something
like a real application

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:01.390
on top of that.

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:03.400
It's just the API
level so that people

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:06.160
can build their own
interfaces or products

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:09.370
or whatever so that they
can connect it in however

00:26:09.370 --> 00:26:10.990
they want.

00:26:10.990 --> 00:26:13.930
GABI FERRARA: You mentioned that
everything-- it's API based.

00:26:13.930 --> 00:26:17.140
And people create their
applications on top of it.

00:26:17.140 --> 00:26:20.230
How does the work on the
perspective of developer

00:26:20.230 --> 00:26:23.260
experience on that, because,
like, everything is on API?

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:26.770
And we know, like, APIs, if
they don't have documentation,

00:26:26.770 --> 00:26:28.300
they don't help much.

00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:30.070
You rely a lot on that.

00:26:30.070 --> 00:26:33.400
And what helps developers
beyond your documentation

00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:36.550
around tooling to help
work with Weaviate?

00:26:36.550 --> 00:26:37.990
LAURA HAM: Yeah,
so documentation

00:26:37.990 --> 00:26:41.210
is a great thing and also a
really big thing to work on,

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:41.710
I would say.

00:26:41.710 --> 00:26:43.250
I'm currently writing it.

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:46.240
But in our documentation, we
have really straightforward

00:26:46.240 --> 00:26:48.280
examples, which
you could directly

00:26:48.280 --> 00:26:50.590
try out in your own
Weaviate cluster

00:26:50.590 --> 00:26:53.000
if you signed up for
one at our website.

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:55.660
And then, we make the API as
straightforward as possible.

00:26:55.660 --> 00:26:58.810
So we have the API--
there's a RESTful API.

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:01.760
And if you want to populate
or fill the database--

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:03.950
you use RESTful API calls.

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:06.310
So all these are
listed on our website

00:27:06.310 --> 00:27:10.790
and explain how you can post
data and update some data.

00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:13.420
And if you want to
query the data--

00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:16.690
so either get something you're
specifically looking for

00:27:16.690 --> 00:27:18.460
or explore the
data, because you're

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:21.010
looking for something you
don't know about or looking

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:25.000
for new relations-- then you're
doing this all via the GraphQL

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:28.700
API, which is designed from
a more human perspective.

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:30.650
So it's based on
natural language.

00:27:30.650 --> 00:27:34.840
And all of this is, of course,
also documented with examples.

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:36.850
